# Citation File Format 1.2.0. GitHub reads this to offer "Cite this
# repository", and Zenodo reads it when archiving a release.
#
# UPDATE `version` AND `date-released` BEFORE TAGGING A RELEASE. They are the
# two fields that go stale silently, and tests/testthat/test-metadata.R holds
# `version` to DESCRIPTION so that half cannot drift unnoticed.
#
# This file and .zenodo.json are excluded from the package tarball via
# .Rbuildignore: CRAN flags non-standard top-level files.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "staleness: Apply and Calibrate Published Signals for Updating Meta-Analyses"
abstract: >-
  Applies the five published statistical signals for whether a systematic
  review or meta-analysis has gone out of date, and backtests them against
  historical evidence. Two of the five are not literal reproductions: where a
  source's own statistic could not distinguish change from no change, a
  calibrated substitute is used, the original quantity is still reported as a
  diagnostic, and the detector is named for the substitution rather than for
  the method it departs from. The backtesting targets are operational
  definitions of the pooled estimate having moved, not recorded decisions by
  review teams, so a reported sensitivity means agreement with a stated
  criterion rather than correctness. A measuring instrument, not an updating
  strategy: it performs no literature searching and no screening.
authors:
  - family-names: "Núñez"
    given-names: "Javier"
    email: "jnverbel@gmail.com"
    affiliation: "Independent researcher"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-9770-4986"
version: 0.2.0
date-released: "2026-08-09"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/jnverbel/staleness"
url: "https://github.com/jnverbel/staleness"
keywords:
  - meta-analysis
  - systematic-review
  - evidence-synthesis
  - research-synthesis
  - reproducibility
  - r
# The preprint describing this work. `preferred-citation` is what GitHub's
# "Cite this repository" and `citation("staleness")` should surface: cite the
# paper, not the package, when reporting a result from it. CFF 1.2.0 has no
# `preprint` type -- the schema's 47 values stop at `article`, `generic` and
# `unpublished` -- so `article` carries it and `notes` says what it really is.
preferred-citation:
  type: article
  title: "Five published signals for updating meta-analyses, applied and evaluated: an exploratory study of when they can be used at all"
  authors:
    - family-names: "Núñez"
      given-names: "Javier"
      email: "jnverbel@gmail.com"
      affiliation: "Independent researcher"
      orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0003-9770-4986"
  year: 2026
  month: 8
  publisher:
    name: "Zenodo"
  doi: "10.5281/zenodo.22050352"
  url: "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22050352"
  notes: "Preprint. Not peer reviewed. 10.5281/zenodo.22050351 is the concept DOI and always resolves to the latest version."
references:
  - type: article
    title: "How quickly do systematic reviews go out of date? A survival analysis"
    authors:
      - family-names: "Shojania"
        given-names: "Kaveh G."
    year: 2007
    doi: "10.7326/0003-4819-147-4-200708210-00179"
    notes: "Source of the Ottawa method, and of the survival estimates this package's motivation rests on."
  - type: article
    title: "Comparison of statistical methods used to detect the need for updating systematic reviews"
    authors:
      - family-names: "Pattanittum"
        given-names: "Porjai"
    year: 2012
    doi: "10.1371/journal.pone.0048894"
    notes: "The only published comparison running all five detectors side by side; its Appendix S3 is a test fixture here."
